The website moves into site/, and the root becomes the node

> I don't wanna change the build system per se, but I need to scoot it into a
> suitable prefix. And I think just the word site is gonna be fine, because
> what we're doing next to it is carving out ways for this thing to act more
> like station node.

This is the room-making change, done before the member-site factory rather than
during it. It is almost entirely a `git mv`.

## What moved

Everything the public gets is now under `site/` — the pages, `_data`,
`_layouts`, `_includes`, `_podcasts`, `_shows`, `assets`, `policies`, `wifi`,
`_headers`, `_redirects`, `robots.txt`, `sitemap.xml`, `staticwebapp.config.json`.

The root keeps everything else: `api/` and `worker/` (services, already beside
the site rather than in it), `script/`, `tests/`, `site-template/`, the
documents, `Gemfile`, `wrangler.jsonc`, `.advocate-engine`.

## The mechanism is one line

```yaml
# _config.yml, still at the root
source: site
```

`_config.yml` deliberately did **not** move down with the site. Jekyll looks for
its config in the directory it is run from, *before* it has read any `source:`
setting — so a config inside the source directory would have to be named on the
command line every time, in CI, at the host, and by hand.

Because it stayed, nothing else had to change:

| | |
|---|---|
| Build command | `bundle exec jekyll build`, unchanged |
| Working directory | repository root, unchanged |
| Output | `_site/` at the root, unchanged |
| `wrangler.jsonc` | unchanged |
| **Cloudflare dashboard** | **not touched** — root `/`, build command and output dir are all still correct |

That last row was the thing worth designing around. The live deploy is
Cloudflare's git integration, configured in their dashboard, which no agent can
change and which nothing in this repository would have told us had gone stale.

## Verified

- **The published bytes are identical.** `origin/main` and this branch were each
  built and the two `_site` trees compared file by file. Every one of the 67
  files matches, modulo the `?v=<mtime>` cache-busting token, which changes
  because `git mv` touched the files.
- **Every Python test passes**: calendar, claim minter, tokens, show proposer,
  catalog report, price list (`--check` too), repricing, feed reader, the
  no-secrets scan, and the member-template round trip.
- **The broker's 119 tests pass.** One real break was found here and fixed:
  `worker/src/index.js` imports the browser's own `assets/js/claims.js` rather
  than keeping a second copy of the claim verifier, and that import crossed the
  boundary the move created.
- **Playwright: 571 passed.** Two failures are this laptop, not the branch —
  `upload.spec.js` shells out to a `python3` with no PyYAML (CI installs it),
  and `smoke.spec.js:78` walks 26 pages against a 30s timeout and takes ~22s
  alone, so it times out under parallel load. Both reproduce on `main`.

## An exclude list disappeared, and that is the point

`_config.yml` carried an `exclude:` of eleven internal documents, because a
`.md` file with no front matter is copied verbatim to a public URL. MANIFEST,
REDIRECTS and RESERVE-DESIGN had each been published that way and taken back;
REVIEW-NOTES.md is the one that mattered — unratified board feedback, verbatim,
one guessable URL from anyone.

The list is gone and nothing replaced it. The documents are at the root, outside
`source:`, so Jekyll cannot see them. The failure mode was *forgetting to add a
file to a list*, and there is no longer a list to forget. `CLAUDE.md` now says
that instead.

## NODE.md, which is the node seat's G2 finally delivered

G2 asked for the consequences of the site being the node to be written down.
They are, and the file carries **a correction to the reasoning recorded on
2026-09-16**, because a later reader deserves it rather than the claim:

> a holding is reachable from `_data`, for free, with no copying step

That is not how Jekyll works. `data_dir` resolves through `sanitized_path`,
which refuses to leave `source:` — **no build root can read its siblings**, and
moving the root does not move the boundary. A symlink crosses it and is a trap:
measured here on 2026-09-17, a symlinked directory in `site/_data/` is read by
an ordinary build and **silently ignored** under `--safe`. No warning, no error,
the page just renders blank.

What being one repository actually buys is better than what was claimed for it:
the five syncs that already exist (`sync-cablecast`, `sync-schedule`,
`sync-calendar`, `sync-feeds`, `sync-nonprofits`) write into `site/_data/` by a
relative path in the same working tree. A node holding is the easiest case of a
pattern the repository has used all along — no network, no key, no submodule pin
to go stale. The sibling-repository arrangement would have made every one of
those five a cross-repository problem.

NODE.md also records what a second site in the cluster would need, so the
member-site factory starts from findings rather than from a blank page. The
sharpest one: **two sites that each get a copy of the header will have two
different headers within a year**, and Jekyll has no cross-source include path,
so a shared layer needs deciding early. A staging step in `script/` matches how
everything else here already works.

## Also recorded, from the same conversation

> FCPM does not need to take cues from station-node about what to keep in its
> library per se. It is definitely up to us what we are bringing over.

This narrows the node seat's G1 and G4 and is written into `advocate.yml` and
`ADVOCATE.md` §6: the prototype is borrowed from for its **shape**; what goes in
the library is FCPM's own decision. "station-node holds it" is not by itself an
argument that FCPM should.

## Deliberately not done

- **Nothing is mounted.** No engine, no submodule, no pin moved. That has been
  out of scope for the node seat since it was seated, and it stays out.
- **No `library/`.** That is G4, and it is waiting on your paragraph about what
  the board decided — escalation `9605`, still open, still blocking nothing else.
- **`site` is singular on purpose.** If a cluster arrives, member sites go under
  `sites/<name>/` rather than renaming this one.

## Worth a look

The path sweep touched prose in ~40 files. Two false positives were caught and
reverted: `_data/site.yml` in `worker/README.md` and `tests/settings.spec.js` is
a path in a **member's** repository, not ours. If you spot another, it will be
of exactly that kind — a path that was never ours to move.
